# meta-deploy.list: the queue meta-deploy.bash runs, in this order.
#
# STANDING RULE (CLAUDE/AgentNotes.md): this list is exactly what needs running NOW. Add a
# run when it is pending, remove it once it has run and is done, leave out what cannot
# usefully run yet, then ask the owner to run meta-deploy.bash. Every step a run needs lives
# in the plan's own scripts, so this is the only command the owner is handed. The order is by
# dependency. A plan with none of triage.bash, deploy.bash and acceptance.bash has nothing
# for the script to do. A triage-only plan is run once, read-only. Words after the folder
# name are passed to that plan's triage.bash, both runs.
#
# One entry per line, four kinds:
#   <plan folder>                  its triage, deploy and acceptance scripts, in order
#   playbooks/<path>.yml           a change no plan owns; run as one unit through its own
#                                  shebang, which goes through run.bash, exactly as
#                                  ./playbooks/imports/<play>.yml does by hand
#   <plan folder>/<script>.bash    one plan script, for a step none of the three fixed names
#                                  fits; run as one unit, like a playbook entry, except
#                                  that a script named *acceptance*.bash may exit 2 for
#                                  COULD NOT ESTABLISH, as an acceptance.bash may
#   changed-plays                  every play that has run here and has to run again, as
#                                  one unit: the checkout's ./run.bash --changed --yes, in
#                                  playbook-main.yml order, stopping at the first failure
#
# changed-plays runs WHERE IT STANDS in the list, like every other entry, and decides which
# plays it runs when it starts, so the entries above it have already run (a play an entry
# above ran is no longer changed, and is not run twice). Put it above the plan entries whose
# deploy or acceptance expects the changed plays to have run, which is the usual case, and
# below an entry it must follow. It replaces a hand-copied list of stale plays: name a
# playbooks/ entry only for a play that must run at a fixed point between two plan steps.
# Its description says which changed plays the agent expects it to pick up and why.
#
# A line starting with # is a comment and is the description of the entry below it: it is how
# the owner learns what a run will do, so write it. Blank lines are ignored. Any other line
# that is not an entry is a fatal error naming its line number.
# Plan 00211 deploy 2 (Task 2.6), the reports and questions rooms, LAST. It needs every team's
# agent sessions restarted since pingbus 0.2.0 was installed, ccy and LXC alike, which the
# host reboot did. A team member that is offline (its container stopped, or its session not
# running) is named as offline and never blocks the run. This runs
# deploy.bash --stage 2: it refuses unless the spike report says every fact holds, then runs
# acceptance.bash --stage 1 again as the pre-flight (the host's and every online declared LXC
# member's pingbus is 0.2.0; an offline member is named, not judged). Then the
# whole play-agent-bus.yml keeps each team's reports and questions rooms (created when
# play-agent-bus.yml passed at 67033c224), the index and the invites; the whole play-claude-yolo.yml finds the ccy image already
# at container 2.79.7. Then acceptance.bash --stage 2 waits up to 180 s for every online
# coordinator to join its questions room and every running ccy seat its reports room, naming
# any that has not (an offline coordinator is named, not waited for), and checks the rooms
# (agent-bus check-rooms prints a coordinator with no live status as OFFLINE and passes; one
# whose session is listening but has not joined is a problem). It
# finds the first team's coordinator in its running ccy session or LXC member; with no team
# coordinator it is REJECTED, named, before it changes anything. It adds and removes one
# throwaway worker that must be refused the questions room and whose test report must reach no
# online inbox. The last run (at b0eb800fa) got further: the worker was taken as a seat, then
# its report was refused, "the team room is not trusted: not joined", because add-member only
# invites and the checker never synced as the worker. A3 now has the worker join by one
# pingbus recv, the sync a real seat's keeper runs, before it reports (Plan 00211 Task 2.11).
# A seat runs beside Element only once you have accepted it at cc --teams' confirm: if A3 says
# that is what refused it, run cc --teams once from a terminal, accept, and run this again; any
# other A3 failure prints pingbus's own error instead. Nothing after A3 has run yet. It also posts a second test report from inside an online LXC member (with none, that
# check is NOT JUDGED), and has the coordinator ask a test question (NOT JUDGED when the
# coordinator is offline). Needs sudo once. Afterwards, if the test question was asked, do the
# Element steps of the plan's ACCEPTANCE-owner.md.
00211-agent-bus-reports-and-questions-rooms/deploy-stage-2.bash
# Plan 00201 Task 2.7 (M7): the automated two-team acceptance again, because its M3 check
# (the session's status line) changed since it last passed, and Plan 00213's cut-over made
# that status line the Go one. The last run (at b0eb800fa) stopped before any check, at
# "session multi's container running: not within 600 s": the base image had just been rebuilt,
# so the headless launch first rebuilt the project image (a 24-step build), and the wait
# counted the build. The launch's waits now start their 600 s again while any ccy image build
# runs (a held build lock, judged as `ccy --build-lock` judges it), with no cap on the build
# itself; a build that fails ends the launch, which still fails at once (Plan 00201 Task
# 2.23). So a first launch after the ccy play may sit through one project image build before
# the checks start, saying once a minute whose build it waits for; if one has hung, see it and
# stop it with `ccy --build-lock` in another terminal. The run
# before that stopped at M3 because the checker still expected
# the Python `exec pingbus status-line`; it now expects exactly what this checkout's seat.py
# writes for a seated session, whose guard runs the kit's pingbus-status-line, and the teams
# drawn as Plan 00217 draws them (`accept201a: <member> (c), ...`, each team in its own colour),
# the form read from this checkout's statusline.py. acceptance.bash only: it makes two throwaway teams declared
# in a run-directory host_vars (your host_vars and teams are never touched), runs one headless
# ccy session on both and a plain one (each one short turn on haiku), checks descriptions,
# launch block, per-session record, status line, `say` and SessionEnd, then purges both teams
# and checks their firewalld rules are gone, however the run ends. Needs sudo once, ccy
# launched interactively in this checkout once (Quick Launch), and the hooks daemon installed.
00201-ccy-teams-many-teams-per-session-descriptions-and-a-picker
# Plan 00214 acceptance (issue #110), LAST, with no changed-plays entry above it: it needs plays
# still to run. Runs no play and needs no sudo. It checks that the health report's
# play-freshness check and run.bash --changed's helper name the same plays (the report said 10
# where --changed ran 25), then runs one collection as host-health.service does (a git fetch,
# and the status document and handoff file rewritten) and checks that fedora-desktop-health and
# the login shell's second reminder of the day print this checkout's run.bash --changed. With
# no play to run it says NOT JUDGED (exit 2). Run it again: its last run (at b0eb800fa) failed
# that second leg with "the collection exited 3", a checker defect: 3 is the collection's
# normal "findings" result, and a stale play is a finding, so the leg could never pass. It now
# accepts 0 and 3, fails on any other status, and fails on 0 when a play is to run (Plan 00214
# Task 4.3c). The run before that found the collection crashing on "'_Sink' object has no
# attribute 'writelines'", fixed since. The collection runs the helper from this checkout, so
# both fixes are live without a play. It is judged only while a play is stale. The
# changed-plays run at b0eb800fa ran every stale play, and nothing a play uses has changed
# since, so NOT JUDGED is the expected result of this run, not a failure: it is judged on the
# first run after a commit changes a play that has run here.
00214-update-all-changed-plays
